{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791818874,"nonce":"4x/5MBexp+SlH56T2TUGZU2C0qY=","title":"Tree allocation 6/7: prove cumulative allocation reduction in hybrid CI","message":"Measure the validated implementation using interleaved baseline/new binaries on the same CI host and the identical benchmark artifact.\n\nRecord per-run correctness, real cumulative allocation, remaining allocation sites, GC counts/time, peak RSS, execution time, compilation/startup cost, and promotion timing. Keep clean timing separate from diagnostic accounting, and include all relevant allocation domains. Publish uncertainty and machine provenance. Tier diagnostics belong in CI logs.\n\nMeasure the fused helper and complete program separately. The retained depth-16 tree is approximately 4 MiB of node storage, an opportunity estimate rather than a promised whole-program allocation target. Hybrid warmup can allocate before optimization; report those bytes. If AOT improves while hybrid does not, address specialization availability/promotion rather than declaring success from AOT alone.\n\nAcceptance:\n- Substantial measured reduction in cumulative allocation for unchanged depth/iterations/checksum, with structural IR evidence supporting the result.\n- Hybrid benefits during the actual benchmark execution; compile/startup and execution costs are included.\n- Accounting uncertainty is reconciled or bounded using work linked to 7e9d728.\n- No allocation reduction claim comes from an arena/accounting change or a cross-machine timing comparison.\n\nCommit measurement/harness changes and retain old/new revision IDs, commands, results, and limitations here.\n\nParent: b002f3fa36f812b67c8cac99068b8183f37520d7e6d61de6caedd54e86bc089f\nDepends on: ab701360f683c8f05f218f4e1ef78fa666a656c9a18022addfdbab879254f542, 3189601a938b594f9aa15556250adc4a3e032f7b128f0389a62194adfab2e4b2","files":null}]}